[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Methodology
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Both libraries are loaded with `libloading` and called **only** through their
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   exported C symbols — the Rust crate is never linked directly, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `#[no_mangle]`/`extern "C"` wrappers are part of what is tested.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Comparison is on **raw bytes** (`common::raw`), never `f32 == f32`, so `-0.0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   vs `+0.0` and differing NaN payloads are caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Every output struct is **pre-poisoned** with a recognisable non-zero pattern
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`common::poison_manifold`), so a field the C leaves untouched is compared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rather than silently agreeing on zeros.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Inputs are property-style randomized from a fixed-seed SplitMix64
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`common::Rng`), over five value families: tame, grid-snapped (to force exact
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   ties, touching and coincidence, which uniform floats never hit), very large,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   very small, and pathological (`±inf`, `NaN`, `±0`, `FLT_MAX`, `FLT_MIN`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   denormals).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `common::scrub_stack()` runs before each FFI call. See the ERRORS.md note on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   rows #37/#41: without it the C's poly path reads our leftover stack bytes and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   its own answer becomes caller-dependent.
